import java.util.Scanner;
import java.util.TreeSet;

public class OptimizedDivisors {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        // Input number
        System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        // Using TreeSet to keep divisors sorted automatically
        TreeSet<Integer> divisors = new TreeSet<>();

        // Loop only till sqrt(n)
        for (int i = 1; i * i <= n; i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) {
                divisors.add(i);        // add divisor
                divisors.add(n / i);    // add pair divisor
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Divisors of " + n + " are: " + divisors);

        sc.close();
    }
}
Enter a number: 28
Divisors of 28 are: [1, 2, 4, 7, 14, 28]

4. Prime Number

Optimal Approach :
A prime number is a number greater than 1 that has only two divisors: 1 and itself.

Examples:

Prime → 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13

Not Prime → 4 (divisible by 2), 9 (divisible by 3)
import java.util.Scanner;

public class PrimeNumber {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        // Input number
        System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        boolean isPrime = true;

        // Prime check: number must be > 1
        if (n <= 1) {
            isPrime = false;
        } else {
            // Check divisibility from 2 to sqrt(n)
            for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
                if (n % i == 0) {
                    isPrime = false;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        // Output result
        if (isPrime) {
            System.out.println(n + " is a Prime number.");
        } else {
            System.out.println(n + " is NOT a Prime number.");
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}

Brute Force Approach :
import java.util.Scanner;

public class PrimeBruteForce {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        // Input number
        System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        boolean isPrime = true;

        // Prime must be greater than 1
        if (n <= 1) {
            isPrime = false;
        } else {
            // Check divisibility from 2 to n-1
            for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
                if (n % i == 0) {
                    isPrime = false;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        // Output result
        if (isPrime) {
            System.out.println(n + " is a Prime number.");
        } else {
            System.out.println(n + " is NOT a Prime number.");
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}


GCD :
The Greatest Common Divisor (GCD) of two numbers is the largest number that divides both of them completely.

Examples:

GCD(12, 18) = 6

GCD(14, 28) = 14

GCD(8, 9) = 1 (co-prime numbers)

Java Program – Brute Force Method

Check all numbers from 1 to min(a, b) and find the largest divisor.

import java.util.Scanner;

public class GCD_BruteForce {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        // Input numbers
        System.out.print("Enter first number: ");
        int a = sc.nextInt();
        System.out.print("Enter second number: ");
        int b = sc.nextInt();

        int gcd = 1;

        // Brute force: check all numbers up to min(a, b)
        for (int i = 1; i <= Math.min(a, b); i++) {
            if (a % i == 0 && b % i == 0) {
                gcd = i;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("GCD of " + a + " and " + b + " is: " + gcd);

        sc.close();
    }
}
Java Program – Euclidean Algorithm (Efficient)
import java.util.Scanner;

public class GCD_Euclidean {
    public static int gcd(int a, int b) {
        // Euclidean algorithm
        while (b != 0) {
            int temp = b;
            b = a % b;
            a = temp;
        }
        return a;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        // Input numbers
        System.out.print("Enter first number: ");
        int a = sc.nextInt();
        System.out.print("Enter second number: ");
        int b = sc.nextInt();

        System.out.println("GCD of " + a + " and " + b + " is: " + gcd(a, b));

        sc.close();
    }
}
